;; The fleet (fleet.yml, bb fleet): one small job that keeps the account's runners full of race
;; and sim-race lanes and gives a runner back the moment any other job waits for one. GitHub has no
;; job priority or preemption: a job queued behind running ones waits until one ends. So the
;; fleet does it: every :fleet/every-s seconds it counts the jobs running and queued across the
;; repository; while another workflow's job is queued and no runner is free, it cancels the newest
;; lane (a lane's cancel still runs its last report and uploads); while nothing waits and runners
;; are free, it dispatches lanes, the kind with fewer first. Lanes on code older than main are
;; cancelled and replaced once they have run :fleet/stale-after-minutes (a merge restarts the
;; rounds, but not every half hour).
{:fleet/cap 20                 ; GitHub Free's concurrent jobs on standard hosted runners
 :fleet/floor {:real 6 :sim 2} ; lanes never given back: the rounds always make progress; six real ones, as a real bot needs ~42 min to the fortress and hours to blaze rods, and lanes above the floor were given back to gym jobs after 2 to 41 min (9 of 13 on 69f1b2ac8, 2026-10-30)
 :fleet/max {:real 8 :sim 8}   ; lanes 1..8 a kind: ci/race.edn's runners and ci/simrace.edn's shares
 :fleet/every-s 60             ; seconds between looks: about four API calls each, inside GITHUB_TOKEN's 1,000 an hour
 :fleet/minutes 340            ; then the job hands over to the next fleet run (GitHub's 6 h job cap)
 :fleet/patience-s 90 ; a job queued less than this is not yet a reason to give a lane back (pages deploys, bursts)
 :fleet/dispatch-grace-s 180 ; a dispatched lane not yet listed still counts this long
 :fleet/cooldown-s 1200 ; after a lane is given back for need, no lane above the floor for this long: lanes dispatched into a gap and cancelled 3 to 20 minutes later reported nothing (2026-10-28, 20 real and 12 sim)
 :fleet/stale-after-minutes 120} ; a lane on replaced code runs this long first: a real run needs about 30 minutes to the Nether, and merges came every 30 (2026-10-17)
